From d2a1974a4f7f13780573d7253806c24691f2ed40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bi Wu <biwu85@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:22:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] vmctx: native VM contexts for the netboot destination --
 execute a program that owns nothing here

The destination half of vmctx on the 6.18 LTS kernel (Intel/VMX): a VM
context is a first-class Linux process whose instructions execute under
hardware virtualization while its memory, files, and operating system
live on the source machine. This kernel provides the vmctx_run and
vmctx_ctl syscalls (context lifecycle, monitor event service, register
and memory access, page take/serve/protect operations, assisted
syscalls run in the context's own task), the process-lifecycle hooks
(fork copies the context and releases it if the fork fails, exec marks
it for rebuild), and the guarded memory-supply rules: a context's
memory is never invented locally, a declined fault that cannot be
served locally is reported again before the context ends, and every
path into a context's memory is accounted.

The executor-side contracts added by the September 2026 audit (builds
#62-#71 of this tree, deployed netbooted as 6.18.35-0-lts-audit-readobj71):

- Architectural CPU state: GETCPU/SETCPU carry the complete state in
  this adapter's own XSAVE layout, TRYFAULT answers address validity,
  and the guest runs the negotiated generic x86 CPU model (CPUID from
  the model, guest XCR0 following it, generic vendor identity). Debug
  exceptions and other exceptions are handed to the source kernel;
  clocks stay with the source.

- Execution CPU time (RUNTIME/BOUNDARY) measured as the guest's own
  nanoseconds, with a native repeating timer forcing checkpoints on
  tickless CPUs; a taken checkpoint is killable and is not a fault
  deadline. Fault cancellation is distinguished from the deadline.

- Lifetime: an initial RESTORE is bound to its monitor's task lifetime
  and released only by explicit RESUME (a five-second attachment
  deadline, then no timeout); the installed AX survives native syscall
  return; retained context descriptors extend to execution contexts
  (owned terminal INFO keeps context and MM identity through reaping,
  mutation controls reject ended lifetimes); execution backing objects
  are retained and replaced under an epoch fence.

- Memory: PROTECT_MM and EXECUTOR_MAP_CAPS (the executor verifies its
  local operations before START), SET_SHARED_PAGE installing only an
  existing folio under the folio lock, MAPOBJ_READ populating reads
  write-protected, mprotect preserving existing write restrictions,
  quiesce leases with PROTECT_BACKING for snapshots, locked page
  transfers drained and failed closed, and only owned private page
  references frozen for transfer.

- The external module (lan-boot vmctx/kernel) invalidates its EPT
  pointer on the executing CPU before every entry and VMCLEARs before
  preemption is re-enabled, so a task never migrates with a VMCS active
  elsewhere.

This machine is disposable by design -- it executes and owns nothing.
Companion userspace and the source-side 7.0.14 tree live in the
lan-boot repository; this tree's delta ships there as kernel-patches/.
